+    <h3>WIDEAWAKE AIRFIELD, ASCENSION → PORT STANLEY · 30 APRIL – 1 MAY 1982</h3>
+    <p>At 2350Z on 30 April 1982, eleven Handley Page Victor K.2 tankers and two Avro Vulcans
+    began lifting off from Ascension Island at one-minute intervals. Their task: deliver a single
+    Vulcan bomber — carrying 21 × 1,000&nbsp;lb bombs — 3,300 nautical miles across the South Atlantic
+    to strike Port Stanley's runway, and bring it home. No air force had ever attempted a bombing
+    raid of this range.</p>
+    <p>Because the Victors had barely more range than the Vulcan, the tankers had to refuel
+    <i>each other</i> in a relay, passing fuel south in a moving chain. The Vulcan was refuelled
+    seven times outbound and once on the return. A failed window seal, a jammed refuelling probe
+    in a thunderstorm, and a tanker flown home on fumes made this the most celebrated refuelling
+    plan in aviation history.</p>
